---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { BRAND_NAME, ORGANIZATION_ID, SITE_URL, WEBSITE_ID } from '../lib/brand';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    '@id': `${SITE_URL}/methodology.html#article`,
    headline: `${BRAND_NAME} BaZi Calculation Methodology`,
    description: 'How MyBaziDestiny calculates the Four Pillars, including solar-term boundaries, true solar time correction, supported dates, and limitations.',
    url: `${SITE_URL}/methodology.html`,
    author: { '@id': ORGANIZATION_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORGANIZATION_ID },
    isPartOf: { '@id': WEBSITE_ID },
    dateModified: '2026-08-02',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="BaZi Calculation Methodology | MyBaziDestiny"
  description="See how MyBaziDestiny calculates BaZi Four Pillars: Li Chun year boundary, Jie solar terms, sexagenary day, Zi hour, true solar time, date ranges, and limitations."
  path="/methodology.html"
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <header class="method-hero wrap-narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="han">算法</span> Methodology</p>
    <h1>How the chart is calculated</h1>
    <p>MyBaziDestiny separates reproducible calendar calculations from traditional interpretation. This page documents the rules used by the current web calculator and the limits users should know.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="method-layout wrap">
    <aside aria-label="Methodology summary">
      <p class="aside-label">At a glance</p>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>Year boundary</dt><dd>Li Chun</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Month boundary</dt><dd>12 Jie terms</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Zi hour</dt><dd>23:00-00:59</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Lunar table</dt><dd>1900-2049</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Solar terms</dt><dd>1900-2100</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </aside>

    <article class="method-body">
      <section>
        <p class="step-label">01 / Four Pillars</p>
        <h2>Year and month use solar-term boundaries</h2>
        <p>The Year Pillar changes at <strong>Li Chun</strong>, the Beginning of Spring, rather than on January 1 or Lunar New Year. A birth before Li Chun remains in the previous sexagenary year for this calculation.</p>
        <p>The Month Pillar changes at the 12 month-starting <strong>Jie solar terms</strong>. It does not switch on the first day of a Gregorian or lunar month.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="step-label">02 / Day and hour</p>
        <h2>Sexagenary day and two-hour branches</h2>
        <p>The Day Pillar is derived with civil-date arithmetic against a fixed sexagenary reference. The implementation uses UTC-based date math to avoid browser daylight-saving shifts changing the entered civil date.</p>
        <p>The Hour Pillar uses the traditional two-hour branches. <strong>Zi hour covers 23:00 through 00:59.</strong> When birth time is unknown, the calculator returns a three-pillar chart and does not invent an Hour Pillar.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="step-label">03 / Location</p>
        <h2>Optional longitude correction</h2>
        <p>When a supported city is selected and true solar time is enabled, the calculator estimates a correction using:</p>
        <pre><code>(city longitude - time-zone standard meridian) x 4 minutes per degree</code></pre>
        <p>This correction can move the effective time across an hour-branch boundary. It is optional and affects the Hour Pillar, not the birth date entered by the user.</p>
        <div class="limitation">
          <h3>Timezone limitation</h3>
          <p>The bundled city list uses static UTC offsets. It does not query a historical timezone or daylight-saving database. For births affected by historical DST, political timezone changes, or an unlisted location, users should confirm the appropriate civil time independently.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="step-label">04 / Supported data</p>
        <h2>Date ranges and approximations</h2>
        <p>The lunar conversion table covers <strong>1900 through 2049</strong>. Solar-term calculation uses mean offsets and is intended for dates from <strong>1900 through 2100</strong>. Mean-term calculations are suitable for this calculator's calendrical boundaries but are not a substitute for a high-precision astronomical ephemeris.</p>
        <p>Reproduce the published boundary outputs on the <a href="/test-cases.html">public BaZi calculator test cases</a> page, or download the same vectors as <a href="/test-cases.json">machine-readable JSON</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="step-label">05 / Interpretation</p>
        <h2>Traditional framework, not scientific prediction</h2>
        <p>Stems, branches, Five Elements, Day Master, compatibility, and alignment guidance come from a traditional Chinese metaphysical framework. These interpretations are cultural and symbolic; they are not scientifically validated predictions of personality, health, wealth, relationships, or future events.</p>
        <p>The current public calculator does not calculate Hidden Stems, weighted chart strength, favorable elements, or personalized Da Yun starting ages.</p>
        <p>Health-related content is general cultural information and is not medical advice. Real decisions should rely on appropriate professional guidance and observable circumstances.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="step-label">06 / Privacy</p>
        <h2>Chart calculation runs in the browser</h2>
        <p>Birth details and chart calculations run locally in the web browser. The tools may use browser storage to preserve user-entered details or paid access on that device. PayPal processes payment information; MyBaziDestiny does not receive card details.</p>
        <p>See the full <a href="/privacy-policy.html">Privacy Policy</a> and current <a href="/pricing.html">official pricing</a>.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .method-hero { padding-top: calc(68px + 5rem); padding-bottom: 4rem; }
  .method-hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 6vw, 4.8rem); margin: 0.55rem 0 1rem; }
  .method-hero > p:last-child { color: var(--bone-dim); font-size: 1.08rem; max-width: 68ch; }
  .method-layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 15rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 5rem; align-items: start; }
  .method-layout > * { min-width: 0; }
  aside { position: sticky; top: 100px; border-top: 1px solid var(--gold); padding-top: 1.2rem; }
  .aside-label, .step-label { color: var(--gold); font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  dl { margin-top: 1rem; }
  dl div { padding: 0.85rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-soft); }
  dt { color: var(--bone-faint); font-size: 0.75rem; }
  dd { color: var(--bone); margin-top: 0.2rem; }
  .method-body { max-width: 760px; }
  .method-body section { padding: 0 0 4.5rem; margin-bottom: 4.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-soft); scroll-margin-top: 90px; }
  .method-body h2 { font-size: 2rem; margin: 0.65rem 0 1.2rem; }
  .method-body p { color: var(--bone-dim); margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.85; }
  pre { margin-top: 1.4rem; padding: 1.2rem; max-width: 100%; overflow-x: auto; white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius-s); color: var(--gold-bright); background: var(--ink-2); }
  .limitation { margin-top: 1.8rem; padding: 1.5rem 0 0 1.5rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--gold-deep); }
  .limitation h3 { font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .limitation p { margin-top: 0.55rem; }
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .method-hero { padding-top: calc(68px + 3.5rem); }
    .method-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 3rem; }
    aside { position: static; }
    dl { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 0 1rem; }
  }
</style>
